{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019wHw975BUTtf9MUVNpGvDc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t### class Hud\n2\t\n3\tPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\n4\tthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\n5\tlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n6\t(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n7\t\n8\tRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n9\t(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\n10\tresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n11\t\n12\t  ctx.hud.clear();\n13\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n14\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n15\t\n16\t- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n17\t- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n18\t- `visible: boolean`\n19\t- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n20\t- `get height: number`\n21\t- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n22\t- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n23\t- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n24\t- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n25\t- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n26\t- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n27\t- `dispose(): void`\n28\t\n29\t### interface HudOptions\n30\t\n31\t- `color?: HudColor`: Default 'white'.\n32\t- `shadow?: HudColor | false`: 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'.\n33\t- `scale?: number`: Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1.\n34\t- `anchor?: HudAnchor`: Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels.\n35\t\n36\t### interface EngineOptions\n37\t\n38\t- `container?: HTMLElement`\n39\t- `resolution?: Resolution`\n40\t- `aspect?: AspectMode`: `adaptive` (default): the art height stays `resolution.height` and the art width follows the screen's aspect (portrait phones fill the screen). `fixed`: always exactly `resolution`, letterboxed. `fill`: adjusts both art dimensions to c\n41\t- `maxFps?: number`: Cap the render loop (simulation keeps its fixed 60 Hz physics step). Default 60, so 120 Hz phones don't run the whole pipeline twice per game frame. 0 = display rate.\n42\t- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow map quality, or `auto` (default: low on touch devices, else medium; lowered once on a slow start).\n43\t- `loadingScreen?: boolean`: Pixel-styled loading indicator while the renderer, physics and assets load. Default true.\n44\t- `mode?: RenderMode`\n45\t- `edges?: EdgeSettings`\n46\t- `camera?: CameraConfig`: Camera preset + parameters. Pick one per game; not meant to change during play.\n47\t- `filters?: readonly string[]`: Post filters (ids from FILTERS) applied in Pixel mode, in order.\n48\t- `look?: Look`: A full look: pixel art or clean rendering and filters per layer (characters & objects, environment) plus whole-scene filters (render/look.ts). Wins over `filters` and `edges`.\n49\t- `seed?: number`: Seed for `ctx.random`: each level gets its own stream from this and the game's name, so the same seed replays the same game. Default 1. `?seed=` in the URL.\n50\t- `record?: number`: Frames of input the engine keeps for each level (`recording()`). Default `MAX_FRAMES`, an hour at 60 fps (up to some 20 MB of JSON with the mouse moving all the time); 0 records nothing.\n51\t- `debugUI?: boolean`: Debug panel. Default: on in dev (`vite`) or with ?debug=1, off in production builds.\n52\t- `debugKeys?: DebugKeysOption`: Engine hotkeys (P Pixel/Raw, R resolution, ` debug panel, [ ] looks, M mute): developer tools. Default: on with the debug panel (dev, ?debug=1), off in a shipped game, whose players have the same options in the settings. `true`\n53\t- `settings?: { button?: TouchCorner | boolean; key?: string | false }`: The player's settings (`engine.settings`): look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate, sound and debug info, in a panel the ⚙ button opens. `button`: its corner (default, or true: '\n54\t- `touch?: boolean`: On-screen joystick + buttons. Default: auto (coarse pointer), or ?touch=1 / ?touch=0.\n55\t- `touchButtons?: readonly TouchButton[]`: On-screen action buttons. Default: the platformer's (DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS).\n56\t- `touchBar?: boolean`: The touch top bar of the engine hotkeys (ⓘ debug panel, P, R, ◐ looks, ♪ mute), on a touch screen while they are on (see `debugKeys`). Default true; false hides it even then, unless the debug panel is on (`?debug=1`, dev builds).\n57\t- `background?: number`\n58\t\n59\t### class Engine\n60\t\n61\t- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n62\t- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n63\t- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listener\n64\t- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the fi\n65\t- `readonly input: Input`\n66\t- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n67\t- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n68\t- `readonly context: GameContext`\n69\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n70\t- `readonly particles: Particles`\n71\t- `readonly hud: Hud`\n72\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n73\t- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n74\t- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n75\t- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n76\t- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.s\n77\t- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n78\t- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n79\t- `frame: number`\n80\t- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n81\t- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n82\t- `time: number`\n83\t- `readonly renderer: View`\n84\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n85\t- `camera: CameraRig`\n86\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n87\t- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n88\t- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n89\t- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n90\t- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n91\t- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the r\n92\t- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (\n93\t- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n94\t- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n95\t- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizo\n96\t- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n97\t- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n98\t- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n99\t- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n100\t- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n101\t- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n102\t- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` wi\n103\t- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n104\t- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n105\t- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n106\t- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n107\t- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n108\t- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n109\t- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n110\t- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n111\t- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n112\t- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agen\n113\t- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n114\t- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n115\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or\n116\t- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n117\t- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n118\t- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n119\t- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on f\n120\t- `snapshot(): Promise<Snapshot>`: The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the game's own state (`Game.snapshot\n121\t- `restore(saved: Snapshot): Promise<void>`: Put the running level back to a snapshot of it: the same game (by name), already loaded; nothing in between is replayed. The level's recording starts again from it (`Recording.from`), so what is played next replays \n122\t- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the simulation: game time, every physics body (exact), `ctx.random`'s state and the game's own (`Game.fingerprint`). Two runs that should be the same (a replay) compare equal. Not the `status()` line: a debug line may carry\n123\t\n124\t### interface Game\n125\t\n126\tA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\n127\tlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n128\t\n129\t- `readonly name: string`\n130\t- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (\n131\t- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n132\t- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n133\t- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n134\t- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before th\n135\t- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n136\t- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n137\t- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n138\t- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n139\t- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a rep\n140\t- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `re\n141\t- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n142\t- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (t\n143\t- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in\n144\t- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, tim\n145\t\n146\t### interface GameContext\n147\t\n148\t- `readonly engine: Engine`\n149\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n150\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n151\t- `readonly input: Input`\n152\t- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n153\t- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n154\t- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n155\t- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n156\t- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n157\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n158\t- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n159\t- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n160\t- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n161\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n162\t\n163\t### interface GamepadLike\n164\t\n165\tThe parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects).\n166\t\n167\t- `readonly connected?: boolean`\n168\t- `readonly mapping?: string`\n169\t- `readonly axes: readonly number[]`\n170\t- `readonly buttons: readonly { readonly pressed: boolean; readonly value?: number }[]`\n171\t\n172\t### interface GameContext\n173\t\n174\t- `readonly engine: Engine`\n175\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n176\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n177\t- `readonly input: Input`\n178\t- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n179\t- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n180\t- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n181\t- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n182\t- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n183\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n184\t- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n185\t- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n186\t- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n187\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n188\t\n189\t### class Input\n190\t\n191\tKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n192\t- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n193\t  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n194\t- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n195\t  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n196\t  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n197\t- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n198\t  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n199\t  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n200\t  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n201\t  a frame is not lost.\n202\t- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n203\t\n204\t- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n205\t- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n206\t- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it n\n207\t- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n208\t- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n209\t- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n210\t- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n211\t- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n212\t- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n213\t- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n214\t- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n215\t- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n216\t- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n217\t- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everyt\n218\t- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n219\t- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n220\t- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n221\t- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n222\t- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n223\t- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n224\t- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n225\t- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n226\t- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n227\t- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n228\t- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped,\n229\t- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n230\t- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n231\t- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n232\t- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n233\t- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n234\t- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n235\t- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n236\t- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n237\t- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n238\t- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n239\t- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n240\t- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. \n241\t- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the \n242\t- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n243\t- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n244\t- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n245\t- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n246\t- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n247\t- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n248\t\n249\t### class AudioManager\n250\t\n251\tWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\n252\tsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\n253\tpersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n254\t\n255\tBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\n256\tfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n257\t(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\n258\twas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n259\t\n260\t- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n261\t- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n262\t- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n263\t- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n264\t- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n265\t- `context: AudioContext | null`\n266\t- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n267\t- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n268\t- `get muted: boolean`\n269\t- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n270\t- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n271\t- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n272\t- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n273\t- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Aud\n274\t- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n275\t- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n276\t- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n277\t- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n278\t- `stopMusic(): void`\n279\t- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n280\t- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n281\t- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n282\t- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n283\t- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n284\t- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n285\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n286\t\n287\t### interface Song\n288\t\n289\tA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\n290\twhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\n291\tEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\n292\tnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n293\t\n294\t- `bpm: number`\n295\t- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n296\t- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n297\t- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n298\t- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n299\t- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n300\t\n301\t### class Timeline\n302\t\n303\t- `new Timeline(o: TimelineOptions)`\n304\t- `readonly duration: number`\n305\t- `time: number`: The playhead (s).\n306\t- `playing: boolean`\n307\t- `speed: number`: Playback rate (1: real time, 0.5: slow motion).\n308\t- `ended: boolean`: True once the end has been reached (by playing or skipping).\n309\t- `readonly fired: string[]`: Cue names in the order they fired (tests and tools).\n310\t- `play(): void`\n311\t- `pause(): void`\n312\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Advance by `dt` seconds of game time (times `speed`) while playing, firing the cues passed.\n313\t- `seek(t: number): void`: Move the playhead to `t` without firing the cues in between (scrubbing). Cues before `t` count as done and cues after it will fire again (going back re-arms them). Ignored while skipping (a cue that loops back cannot undo a skip).\n314\t- `skip(): void`: Jump to the end: the cues not yet fired run (except the cosmetic ones), then `onEnd`. Cues after `duration` never fire, played or skipped.\n315\t- `camera(t = this.time): CameraShot | null`: Where the camera is at `t` (default now): on each key at its time, gliding between keys on a smooth curve (Catmull-Rom tangents, so the speed has no jolt at a key; the first and last key of each shot ease in and ou\n316\t- `line(t = this.time): Line | null`: The subtitle showing at `t` (default now), or null.\n317\t- `bars(t = this.time): number`: How far the letterbox bars are in (0..1) at `t` (default now): sliding in at the start, out at the end.\n318\t\n319\t### applySky()\n320\t\n321\t```ts\n322\tfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n323\t```\n324\t\n325\tPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n326\t\n327\t### skyAt()\n328\t\n329\t```ts\n330\tfunction skyAt(hour: number): SkyState\n331\t```\n332\t\n333\tThe sky at `hour` (wraps around 24).\n334\t\n335\t### class Precipitation\n336\t\n337\t- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n338\t- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n339\t- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n340\t- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n341\t- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n342\t- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n343\t- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n344\t- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n345\t- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n346\t\n347\t### class WaterSurface\n348\t\n349\t- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n350\t- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n351\t- `waves: Wave[]`\n352\t- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n353\t- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n354\t- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n355\t- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n356\t- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n357\t- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n358\t- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n359\t- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n360\t\n361\t### class Boids\n362\t\n363\t- `new Boids(count: number, o: BoidOptions = {})`\n364\t- `readonly count: number`\n365\t- `readonly pos: Float32Array`\n366\t- `readonly vel: Float32Array`\n367\t- `o: Required<Omit<BoidOptions, 'bounds' | 'seed'>> & { bounds: BoidOptions['bounds'] }`\n368\t- `seek: Vec3 | null`: A point everyone is drawn toward (null: none).\n369\t- `seekWeight: number`\n370\t- `flee: { at: Vec3; radius: number } | null`: A threat to scatter from.\n371\t- `fleeWeight: number`\n372\t- `obstacles: { x: number; y: number; z: number; r: number }[]`: Spheres to steer around (x, y, z, r).\n373\t- `spacing(): number`: Average distance to the nearest neighbour (tests, tools).\n374\t- `order(): { speed: number; alignment: number }`: Average speed and how lined up the headings are (0 random, 1 all the same way).\n375\t- `step(dt: number): void`\n376\t\n377\t### interface ClipDef\n378\t\n379\t- `name: string`\n380\t- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n381\t- `loop?: boolean`\n382\t- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n383\t- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n384\t- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instea\n385\t- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n386\t- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n387\t- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n388\t- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n389\t- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n390\t- `notes?: string`\n391\t\n392\t### compileClips()\n393\t\n394\t```ts\n395\tfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n396\t```\n397\t\n398\t### analyzeClip()\n399\t\n400\t```ts\n401\tfunction analyzeClip(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip): ClipReport\n402\t```\n403\t\n404\t### clothGrid()\n405\t\n406\t```ts\n407\tfunction clothGrid(o: ClothOptions): VerletBody & { cols: number; rows: number; uv: Float32Array }\n408\t```\n409\t\n410\tA sheet of cloth: a grid of particles with structural, shear and bend edges, two triangles per cell.\n411\t\n412\t### class SoftMesh\n413\t\n414\t- `new SoftMesh(body: VerletBody, material: Material, o: { uv?: Float32Array; doubleSided?: boolean } = {})`\n415\t- `readonly body: VerletBody`\n416\t- `sync(): void`: Copy the particles into the mesh (call once per frame after stepping the body).\n417\t\n418\t### class Trail\n419\t\n420\t- `new Trail(o: TrailOptions = {})`\n421\t- `readonly capacity: number`\n422\t- `width: number`\n423\t- `life: number`\n424\t- `readonly head: Color`: Head and tail colours (live: `trail.head.setHex(...)`).\n425\t- `readonly tail: Color`\n426\t- `push(p: Vector3, time: number): void`: The trail's newest point (call every frame while it moves).\n427\t- `reset(): void`: Drop every point.\n428\t- `get length: number`: Points alive now.\n429\t- `update(time: number, camera: Camera): void`: Per frame: forget old points, rebuild the ribbon facing `camera`.\n430\t- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n431\t\n432\t### class Tweens\n433\t\n434\tA set of running tweens (the engine keeps one per game: `ctx.tweens`).\n435\t\n436\t- `get count: number`: Tweens still running.\n437\t- `to(target: T, to: Partial<Record<keyof T, number>>, options: TweenOptions = {}): Tween`: Glide `target`'s numeric properties to `to`. A new tween on the same target and property takes over (the old one stops where it is).\n438\t- `value(from: number, to: number, options: TweenOptions, apply: (v: number) => void): Tween`: Tween a plain number from `from` to `to`, calling `apply(v)` every frame.\n439\t- `call(seconds: number, fn: () => void): Tween`: Call `fn` after `seconds` of game time.\n440\t- `cancel(target?: object): void`: Stop every tween on `target` (or everything).\n441\t- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (level unload). Pending `done` promises resolve.\n442\t- `update(dt: number): void`\n443\t\n444\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c060cbf0-9de2-4b69-aed6-69ef0ff7f0ee","uuid":"cc40991e-ca8c-47c3-8639-b20cacbba746","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:20:19.959Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-171907--x89353d-work/c060cbf0-9de2-4b69-aed6-69ef0ff7f0ee/tool-results/bc2ly9kq8.txt","content":"### class Hud\n\nPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\nthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\nlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n\nRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\nresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n\n  ctx.hud.clear();\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n\n- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n- `visible: boolean`\n- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n- `get height: number`\n- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### interface HudOptions\n\n- `color?: HudColor`: Default 'white'.\n- `shadow?: HudColor | false`: 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'.\n- `scale?: number`: Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1.\n- `anchor?: HudAnchor`: Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels.\n\n### interface EngineOptions\n\n- `container?: HTMLElement`\n- `resolution?: Resolution`\n- `aspect?: AspectMode`: `adaptive` (default): the art height stays `resolution.height` and the art width follows the screen's aspect (portrait phones fill the screen). `fixed`: always exactly `resolution`, letterboxed. `fill`: adjusts both art dimensions to c\n- `maxFps?: number`: Cap the render loop (simulation keeps its fixed 60 Hz physics step). Default 60, so 120 Hz phones don't run the whole pipeline twice per game frame. 0 = display rate.\n- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow map quality, or `auto` (default: low on touch devices, else medium; lowered once on a slow start).\n- `loadingScreen?: boolean`: Pixel-styled loading indicator while the renderer, physics and assets load. Default true.\n- `mode?: RenderMode`\n- `edges?: EdgeSettings`\n- `camera?: CameraConfig`: Camera preset + parameters. Pick one per game; not meant to change during play.\n- `filters?: readonly string[]`: Post filters (ids from FILTERS) applied in Pixel mode, in order.\n- `look?: Look`: A full look: pixel art or clean rendering and filters per layer (characters & objects, environment) plus whole-scene filters (render/look.ts). Wins over `filters` and `edges`.\n- `seed?: number`: Seed for `ctx.random`: each level gets its own stream from this and the game's name, so the same seed replays the same game. Default 1. `?seed=` in the URL.\n- `record?: number`: Frames of input the engine keeps for each level (`recording()`). Default `MAX_FRAMES`, an hour at 60 fps (up to some 20 MB of JSON with the mouse moving all the time); 0 records nothing.\n- `debugUI?: boolean`: Debug panel. Default: on in dev (`vite`) or with ?debug=1, off in production builds.\n- `debugKeys?: DebugKeysOption`: Engine hotkeys (P Pixel/Raw, R resolution, ` debug panel, [ ] looks, M mute): developer tools. Default: on with the debug panel (dev, ?debug=1), off in a shipped game, whose players have the same options in the settings. `true`\n- `settings?: { button?: TouchCorner | boolean; key?: string | false }`: The player's settings (`engine.settings`): look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate, sound and debug info, in a panel the ⚙ button opens. `button`: its corner (default, or true: '\n- `touch?: boolean`: On-screen joystick + buttons. Default: auto (coarse pointer), or ?touch=1 / ?touch=0.\n- `touchButtons?: readonly TouchButton[]`: On-screen action buttons. Default: the platformer's (DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS).\n- `touchBar?: boolean`: The touch top bar of the engine hotkeys (ⓘ debug panel, P, R, ◐ looks, ♪ mute), on a touch screen while they are on (see `debugKeys`). Default true; false hides it even then, unless the debug panel is on (`?debug=1`, dev builds).\n- `background?: number`\n\n### class Engine\n\n- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listener\n- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the fi\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n- `readonly context: GameContext`\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n- `readonly particles: Particles`\n- `readonly hud: Hud`\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.s\n- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n- `frame: number`\n- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n- `time: number`\n- `readonly renderer: View`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the r\n- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (\n- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizo\n- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` wi\n- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agen\n- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or\n- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on f\n- `snapshot(): Promise<Snapshot>`: The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the game's own state (`Game.snapshot\n- `restore(saved: Snapshot): Promise<void>`: Put the running level back to a snapshot of it: the same game (by name), already loaded; nothing in between is replayed. The level's recording starts again from it (`Recording.from`), so what is played next replays \n- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the simulation: game time, every physics body (exact), `ctx.random`'s state and the game's own (`Game.fingerprint`). Two runs that should be the same (a replay) compare equal. Not the `status()` line: a debug line may carry\n\n### interface Game\n\nA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\nlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (\n- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before th\n- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a rep\n- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `re\n- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (t\n- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in\n- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, tim\n\n### interface GameContext\n\n- `readonly engine: Engine`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n\n### interface GamepadLike\n\nThe parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects).\n\n- `readonly connected?: boolean`\n- `readonly mapping?: string`\n- `readonly axes: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly buttons: readonly { readonly pressed: boolean; readonly value?: number }[]`\n\n### interface GameContext\n\n- `readonly engine: Engine`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n\n### class Input\n\nKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n  a frame is not lost.\n- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n\n- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it n\n- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everyt\n- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped,\n- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. \n- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the \n- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n\n### class AudioManager\n\nWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\nsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\npersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n\nBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\nfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\nwas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n\n- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n- `context: AudioContext | null`\n- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n- `get muted: boolean`\n- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Aud\n- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n- `stopMusic(): void`\n- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n\n### interface Song\n\nA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\nwhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\nEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\nnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n\n- `bpm: number`\n- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n\n### class Timeline\n\n- `new Timeline(o: TimelineOptions)`\n- `readonly duration: number`\n- `time: number`: The playhead (s).\n- `playing: boolean`\n- `speed: number`: Playback rate (1: real time, 0.5: slow motion).\n- `ended: boolean`: True once the end has been reached (by playing or skipping).\n- `readonly fired: string[]`: Cue names in the order they fired (tests and tools).\n- `play(): void`\n- `pause(): void`\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Advance by `dt` seconds of game time (times `speed`) while playing, firing the cues passed.\n- `seek(t: number): void`: Move the playhead to `t` without firing the cues in between (scrubbing). Cues before `t` count as done and cues after it will fire again (going back re-arms them). Ignored while skipping (a cue that loops back cannot undo a skip).\n- `skip(): void`: Jump to the end: the cues not yet fired run (except the cosmetic ones), then `onEnd`. Cues after `duration` never fire, played or skipped.\n- `camera(t = this.time): CameraShot | null`: Where the camera is at `t` (default now): on each key at its time, gliding between keys on a smooth curve (Catmull-Rom tangents, so the speed has no jolt at a key; the first and last key of each shot ease in and ou\n- `line(t = this.time): Line | null`: The subtitle showing at `t` (default now), or null.\n- `bars(t = this.time): number`: How far the letterbox bars are in (0..1) at `t` (default now): sliding in at the start, out at the end.\n\n### applySky()\n\n```ts\nfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n```\n\nPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n\n### skyAt()\n\n```ts\nfunction skyAt(hour: number): SkyState\n```\n\nThe sky at `hour` (wraps around 24).\n\n### class Precipitation\n\n- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n\n### class WaterSurface\n\n- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n- `waves: Wave[]`\n- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n\n### class Boids\n\n- `new Boids(count: number, o: BoidOptions = {})`\n- `readonly count: number`\n- `readonly pos: Float32Array`\n- `readonly vel: Float32Array`\n- `o: Required<Omit<BoidOptions, 'bounds' | 'seed'>> & { bounds: BoidOptions['bounds'] }`\n- `seek: Vec3 | null`: A point everyone is drawn toward (null: none).\n- `seekWeight: number`\n- `flee: { at: Vec3; radius: number } | null`: A threat to scatter from.\n- `fleeWeight: number`\n- `obstacles: { x: number; y: number; z: number; r: number }[]`: Spheres to steer around (x, y, z, r).\n- `spacing(): number`: Average distance to the nearest neighbour (tests, tools).\n- `order(): { speed: number; alignment: number }`: Average speed and how lined up the headings are (0 random, 1 all the same way).\n- `step(dt: number): void`\n\n### interface ClipDef\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n- `loop?: boolean`\n- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instea\n- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n- `notes?: string`\n\n### compileClips()\n\n```ts\nfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n```\n\n### analyzeClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction analyzeClip(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip): ClipReport\n```\n\n### clothGrid()\n\n```ts\nfunction clothGrid(o: ClothOptions): VerletBody & { cols: number; rows: number; uv: Float32Array }\n```\n\nA sheet of cloth: a grid of particles with structural, shear and bend edges, two triangles per cell.\n\n### class SoftMesh\n\n- `new SoftMesh(body: VerletBody, material: Material, o: { uv?: Float32Array; doubleSided?: boolean } = {})`\n- `readonly body: VerletBody`\n- `sync(): void`: Copy the particles into the mesh (call once per frame after stepping the body).\n\n### class Trail\n\n- `new Trail(o: TrailOptions = {})`\n- `readonly capacity: number`\n- `width: number`\n- `life: number`\n- `readonly head: Color`: Head and tail colours (live: `trail.head.setHex(...)`).\n- `readonly tail: Color`\n- `push(p: Vector3, time: number): void`: The trail's newest point (call every frame while it moves).\n- `reset(): void`: Drop every point.\n- `get length: number`: Points alive now.\n- `update(time: number, camera: Camera): void`: Per frame: forget old points, rebuild the ribbon facing `camera`.\n- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n\n### class Tweens\n\nA set of running tweens (the engine keeps one per game: `ctx.tweens`).\n\n- `get count: number`: Tweens still running.\n- `to(target: T, to: Partial<Record<keyof T, number>>, options: TweenOptions = {}): Tween`: Glide `target`'s numeric properties to `to`. A new tween on the same target and property takes over (the old one stops where it is).\n- `value(from: number, to: number, options: TweenOptions, apply: (v: number) => void): Tween`: Tween a plain number from `from` to `to`, calling `apply(v)` every frame.\n- `call(seconds: number, fn: () => void): Tween`: Call `fn` after `seconds` of game time.\n- `cancel(target?: object): void`: Stop every tween on `target` (or everything).\n- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (level unload). Pending `done` promises resolve.\n- `update(dt: number): void`\n\n","numLines":444,"startLine":1,"totalLines":444}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019wHw975BUTtf9MUVNpGvDc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkiYpk2miX33RBqzorxu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CSRESD2QhrSbKagcXmtzTK","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"A=src/API.md; grep -n \"^### \" $A | grep -i -E \"input|audio|song|music|clip|screen|tween|particle|light|spatial|gamepad|hero|Rng|Random\" | head -60; grep -n \"^## \" $A","description":"Find remaining API section names"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1833,"cache_read_input_tokens":147271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1833},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c060cbf0-9de2-4b69-aed6-69ef0ff7f0ee","uuid":"b292a5cd-7251-40db-ac59-bc5cf06799b6","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:20:20.813Z","request_id":"req_011CgkiYpX8jpuTLLMjZU1MZ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01CSRESD2QhrSbKagcXmtzTK":{"command":"A=src/API.md; grep -n \"^### \" $A | grep -i -E \"input|audio|song|music|clip|screen|tween|particle|light|spatial|gamepad|hero|Rng|Random\" | head -60; grep -n \"^## \" $A","description":"Find remaining API section names"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CSRESD2QhrSbKagcXmtzTK","type":"tool_result","content":"312:### class LoadingScreen\n1203:### LIGHT_POOL_SIZES\n1211:### class LightAssigner\n1227:### interface LightHandle\n1249:### class LightPool\n1267:### interface LightPoolOptions\n1272:### interface LightPoolStats\n1281:### interface LightRequestOptions\n1569:### interface DriveInput\n2156:### interface MoveInput\n2231:### readMoveInput()\n2268:### GAMEPAD_BUTTONS\n2278:### interface GamepadLike\n2287:### class Input\n2347:### interface InputFrame\n2367:### interface InputState\n2462:### class AudioManager\n2500:### interface AudioSettings\n2536:### parseSong()\n2544:### PLAYGROUND_SONG\n2582:### interface Song\n2614:### interface Spatial\n2620:### spatialize()\n2628:### interface SpatialOptions\n2678:### class ParticlePool\n2703:### interface ParticlePreset\n2721:### type ParticlePresetName\n2727:### class Particles\n2751:### PARTICLES\n3149:### interface ModelLight\n3157:### interface ModelLightRequest\n3541:### analyzeClip()\n3547:### interface ClipDef\n3562:### interface ClipReport\n3587:### compileClip()\n3597:### compileClips()\n3634:### gaitClip()\n3778:### sampleClip()\n3844:### validateClip()\n3854:### HERO_CLIPS\n3860:### HERO_MODEL\n3866:### HERO_RAGDOLL\n3876:### HERO_RIG\n4651:### class Rng\n4681:### SCREEN_LAYER\n4689:### class ScreenFx\n4703:### interface ScreenFxState\n5007:### interface Tween\n5013:### interface TweenOptions\n5023:### class Tweens\n5:## Sections\n23:## 1. Engine and games\n542:## 2. Camera\n677:## 3. Rendering, looks and filters\n1146:## 4. Materials, meshes and lights\n1387:## 5. Physics\n2132:## 6. Characters\n2240:## 7. Input and touch\n2452:## 8. Audio\n2668:## 9. Particles\n2760:## 10. HUD\n2820:## 11. Models and textures (ASSETS.md)\n3539:## 12. Animation toolkit\n3852:## 13. Hero kit\n3895:## 14. three.js and Rapier\n3909:## 15. More","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0