{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkrYDvwpyqVLVZzoh2kc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Grandpa Turismo is built and handed in.** All eight test suites pass (849 checks), including a scripted playthrough of the whole career and all three hub conformance runs.\n\n- **Not tested on real hardware:** nobody has driven it on a real pad, TV or speakers, so how the handling feels is still unproven. I tested with software rendering (10–15 fps), stubbed pads and audio analysis.\n- **Not re-checked by eye:** my last fixes passed the tests, but I didn't take new screenshots of them. They are the wheel shape, narrow-screen text and prompts, and replay camera positions.\n- **Departures from the plan:**\n  - I lowered AI pace ranges and raised prizes, as the plan invited, because the first bot runs couldn't afford or win the later events.\n  - I capped tyre wear from sliding, because fast cars were wearing out their tyres and crashing on Saltline.\n  - The playthrough bot drives at 0.97 skill, not 1.0, because the AI overdrives tuned cars at full pace.\n- **Engine:** `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the released kit. The hub client is pasted in unmodified.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 17:19:07\n- **Source:** Gran Turismo 3: A-Spec\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** racing simulation with a career, tuning and car collecting. Third-person chase camera (near and far), bonnet view and TV cameras in replays. Menus sit on a 3D diorama seen from a slow three-quarter camera.\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 as it ships (three.js r186 WebGPU, Rapier), plain JavaScript modules, esbuild 0.28.2 to one HTML, AI Hub session client 1.4.0 unmodified, no CDN at runtime. The four-wheel car model is plain JS from the plan, not the engine's `Vehicle`.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel look: low-poly toon meshes, the 16-colour palette, pixel outlines, 480×270. It has 14 original cars, six dressed circuits, a peninsula diorama and a set for every menu.\n- **Animation:** the engine's rig, `ClipDef` and `compileClips`, with ten new clips (pit crew, marshal, examiner, dealer, mechanic, podium, crowd). Also procedural body motion, wheels, turntables, dyno, car wash, `Timeline` podium and prize scenes, and cloth flags.\n- **Audio:** all in code, no files. A Web Audio engine synth per car, tyre, wind, kerb and gravel noise, synthesised effects, eight original songs with music at 10% by default, and gibberish voices for four speakers with subtitles.\n- **Online:** AI Hub protocol v1 in ghost mode (1–4 players, own card). On its own: Couch Duel split screen and pass-the-pad time trial. No other network use.\n- **Testing:** headless tools and scripts allowed, one command printing failures and a one-line count, a scripted playthrough of the whole game, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Stand-ins were pad stubs, a touch profile, Web Audio render and analyser, software WebGPU, and the hub harness on an OS-picked port. WebKit has no WebGPU here, so it couldn't be run.\n- **Research:** none on the web. I read the kit's GUIDE.md and ASSETS.md, parts of API.md, the hub README and the plan.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 20. Examples: rolling starts never went green, AI never pitted because of that, a pause menu that opened and closed in one frame, the idle timer counting frames instead of seconds, attract mode unable to exit during a load, all AI cars the same paint, the start gantry turned 90° across the road, a tyre wear spiral on the banked track, and prizes too low to afford the cars the events asked for.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 5: a name clash that stopped the race scene loading, a model id clash that stopped the game booting, rolling starts that held every race at 90 km/h, attract mode that couldn't be left during a load, and a wear spiral that left fast cars unable to finish on Saltline.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The whole-game playthrough bot. It found the money and balance problems, the wear spiral and every broken screen transition, and it now finishes the career with no dev tools (day 26 when I last logged it).\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The spec-sheet check of 0–100 and top speed. The code came from the plan and it never failed, so it only confirmed what the lap regression already showed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** `Engine.headless`. The real game code ran in Node with the same scenes and menus, so a bot could play the whole career in minutes. The pixel look also makes plain low-poly data look good with very little work.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes.\n  - Naming a field `setup` on a game object overwrote the setup hook and gave only \"e.setup is not a function\".\n  - A game's model id clashing with a library id throws at import.\n  - I guessed `sawtooth` for a wave, but the engine's name is `saw`.\n  - Errors print the one-line minified engine source, so I needed a small wrapper to read a stack.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The car model and race simulation (from the plan), then the 10-foot UI: focus, prompts and glyphs through one immediate-mode layer over the HUD, for pad, keyboard, mouse and touch.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Input outside the engine's model. Analog triggers and several pads aren't exposed, so I polled pads myself, sent them through `input.send`, and latched commands once a frame across `fixedUpdate` and `update`. Software WebGPU at 10–15 fps also made browser tests slow and timing-sensitive.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Expose analog gamepad state per pad (triggers, axes, pad index) through `Input`, so racing games and split screen don't need their own polling and command latching.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The honest structure: a detailed plan with proven facts and tested code, and a brief that spells out what to hand in and how to test. The rule of one test command that prints only failures saved a lot of noise.\n- **What was confusing?** Very little. The plan says the play bot uses skill 1.0, but `aiDrive` overdrives tuned cars at that pace, so I ran it at 0.97. The `hub:players` meta (1-2) and the exit interview's players field (1–4) count different things, which the plan does explain.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Balance without a human. Pace, prices and medals could only be judged by a bot, so how the handling feels on a real pad is still unproven. Keeping 12 tests, 22 races and every set-piece working together was the largest single job.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Allow a slightly longer list of proven AI behaviour for tuned cars, and say up front how good a human the balance should assume, because that number decides every pace range.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in `work/`:\n- `GrandpaTurismo-GranTurismo3ASpec-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-171907.html` (the game, 5.5 MB; runs from `file://`)\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `GrandpaTurismo-GranTurismo3ASpec-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-171907/` (dev files)\n\nFrom the dev folder, `node tools/test.mjs` runs the tests, `node tools/build.mjs` rebuilds the game, and `docs/adding-content.md` explains how to add cars, tracks, tests, events and songs."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":907,"cache_read_input_tokens":603142,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":907},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c060cbf0-9de2-4b69-aed6-69ef0ff7f0ee","uuid":"b16d7080-8ec7-4e46-aca4-884a49ad15ce","timestamp":"2026-11-06T02:05:27.064Z","request_id":"req_011CgkrYDccBnuMQJhKobsCV"}
